Networking: SDD

Purpose:
  Define networking patterns, CDN configuration, WAF rules, load
  balancer types, API gateway responsibilities, service mesh guidance,
  DNS management, TLS requirements, network security, and firewall
  rules. Networking is the foundation for all inter-component and
  external communication.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  CDN
  WAF
  Load balancer
  API gateway
  Service mesh
  DNS
  TLS
  Network security
  Firewall rules

Principles:
  All external traffic MUST be encrypted with TLS.
  Network security MUST follow zero-trust principles.
  Firewall rules MUST be deny-by-default.
  DNS MUST use low TTL for failover-capable records.
  All inter-service communication MUST use DNS names, never IPs.

CDN:

  description: Content Delivery Network for static assets and API caching
  when_to_use:
    - L1 and above
    - static assets (images, JS, CSS, video)
    - global or regional user base
    - DDoS protection needed

  configuration:
    cache_rules:
      - immutable assets: 1 year TTL with content hash
      - versioned assets: 1 year TTL
      - HTML: no cache or short TTL
      - API responses: respect Cache-Control headers

    origin_security:
      - origin MUST allow only CDN IP ranges
      - origin shield for cache hit ratio improvement

  benefits:
    - reduced origin load
    - lower user latency
    - DDoS absorption at edge
    - geographic distribution

WAF:

  description: Web Application Firewall for HTTP/HTTPS traffic
  rules:
    owasp_top10:
      - SQL injection
      - XSS
      - CSRF
      - SSRF
      - broken authentication
      - sensitive data exposure
      - security misconfiguration

    rate_limiting:
      - per IP: prevent brute force
      - per endpoint: prevent abuse
      - per user: account-specific limits

    geo_blocking:
      - block high-risk regions
      - allow only expected traffic origins

  placement:
    edge: Cloudflare, AWS WAF (global protection)
    ingress: NGINX, Envoy (application-level rules)

LoadBalancer:

  types:
    L4:
      description: Transport layer (TCP, UDP)
      use_when:
        - non-HTTP protocols
        - maximum performance needed
        - TLS passthrough required

    L7:
      description: Application layer (HTTP, HTTPS, gRPC)
      use_when:
        - HTTP services
        - path-based routing
        - header-based routing
        - TLS termination

  configuration:
    health_checks:
      - active: periodic probe with configurable interval
      - passive: monitor response errors and timeouts
      - slow_start: gradual traffic ramp for new instances

    algorithms:
      round_robin: equal capacity nodes
      least_connections: variable request duration
      weighted: heterogeneous node capacity
      ip_hash: session affinity (avoid when possible)

    high_availability:
      - deploy at least 2 load balancers
      - use anycast or DNS failover
      - health check MUST be independent of service health

APIGateway:

  description: Entry point for external API traffic
  responsibilities:
    routing:
      - path-based routing to services
      - version routing (/v1/, /v2/)

    authentication:
      - API key validation
      - JWT validation
      - OAuth2 flow orchestration

    rate_limiting:
      - per client rate limits
      - burst handling
      - quota enforcement

    transformation:
      - request/response transformation
      - protocol translation

    documentation:
      - OpenAPI spec serving
      - developer portal

  when_to_use:
    - external API exposure
    - multiple client types
    - unified authentication layer
    - L2 and above

  anti_patterns:
    gateway_as_bottleneck:
      description: Gateway becomes single point of failure
      rule: deploy multiple gateway instances

    business_logic_in_gateway:
      description: Moving business logic to gateway
      rule: gateway MUST handle only cross-cutting concerns

ServiceMesh:

  description: Infrastructure layer for service-to-service communication
  components:
    sidecar_proxy: intercepts all service traffic
    control_plane: manages proxy configuration
    data_plane: proxies handling traffic

  capabilities:
    traffic_management:
      - retries, timeouts, circuit breaking
      - load balancing
      - traffic splitting (canary)

    security:
      - mTLS between services
      - certificate management
      - access control policies

    observability:
      - distributed tracing
      - metrics
      - access logging

  when_to_use:
    - L3-L5
    - many microservices
    - complex traffic patterns
    - strong security requirements

  when_to_avoid:
    - L0-L2
    - monolithic architecture
    - small number of services
    - insufficient operational maturity

DNS:

  management:
    internal_dns:
      - service discovery
      - consistent naming convention
      - TTL: 60 seconds for dynamic services

    external_dns:
      - domain registrar with low TTL (300s) for failover
      - health-checked DNS for critical endpoints
      - geographic DNS for multi-region

  anti_patterns:
    hardcoded_ips:
      description: Using IP addresses instead of DNS names
      rule: all inter-service communication MUST use DNS names

    long_ttl:
      description: Long TTL preventing failover
      rule: critical services MUST have TTL <= 60 seconds

TLS:

  requirements:
    external_traffic:
      - all external HTTP MUST use HTTPS
      - TLS 1.3 minimum
      - strong cipher suites only
      - certificate rotation automated

    internal_traffic:
      - mTLS for service mesh
      - TLS for sensitive internal APIs
      - certificate rotation automated

  certificate_management:
    - use managed certificate service (ACM, Let's Encrypt)
    - certificate expiry monitoring
    - automated renewal before expiry

NetworkSecurity:

  zero_trust:
    description: Never trust, always verify
    principles:
      - authenticate every request
      - authorize minimum required access
      - encrypt all traffic
      - inspect all traffic
      - log all access

  segmentation:
    - separate networks by sensitivity
    - restrict cross-network traffic
    - use private subnets for internal services
    - public subnets only for load balancers

  DDoS:
    - CDN for DDoS absorption
    - rate limiting at edge
    - traffic scrubbing services
    - capacity overprovisioning

FirewallRules:

  principles:
    deny_by_default:
      - default deny all inbound
      - explicit allow for required ports
      - explicit deny for known bad IPs

    least_privilege:
      - allow only required ports
      - allow only required source IPs
      - restrict database access to application tier only

    outbound:
      - restrict outbound to required destinations
      - monitor unexpected outbound traffic
      - block known malicious destinations

  configuration:
    - firewall rules MUST be version controlled
    - firewall rules MUST be reviewed on change
    - firewall rule changes MUST be audited

AntiPatterns:

  OpenFirewall:
    description: Wide open firewall rules
    rule: MUST deny by default, allow explicitly

  UnencryptedInternal:
    description: Internal traffic without encryption
    rule: sensitive internal traffic MUST be encrypted

  IPHardcoding:
    description: Hardcoded IP addresses in configuration
    rule: MUST use DNS names with TTL

  SingleRegion:
    description: Production in single region without network redundancy
    rule: L99.95 and above MUST span regions

  MonitoringBlindSpots:
    description: No visibility into east-west traffic
    rule: internal traffic MUST be monitored

Navigation:
  Levels: @levels.sdd
  Patterns: @patterns.sdd
  Scaling: @scaling.sdd
  Availability: @availability.sdd
  Observability: @observability.sdd
  Deployment: @deployment.sdd
  Project: @project/project.sdd
  ProjectArchitecture: @projects/{project_name}/architecture/INDEX.sdd
  Decisions: @projects/{project_name}/decisions/INDEX.sdd

